    {
        "title": "UTIs in Women: Treatment, Recurrence, and the Cranberry Question",
        "slug": "uti-treatment-recurrence", "section": "conditions",
        "subcategory": "Women's Health",
        "summary": "More than half of women will have a urinary tract infection. For the unlucky group that gets them repeatedly, prevention has an evidence base worth knowing.",
        "body_html": "<p>The burning, urgency, and every-ten-minutes trips to the bathroom of a bladder infection are hard to mistake. Uncomplicated UTIs respond to short, targeted antibiotic courses — often just three to five days of first-line agents such as nitrofurantoin — and symptoms usually ease within a day or two of starting. Drink water generously, and use acetaminophen or ibuprofen for discomfort while the antibiotic works.</p><p>Know the escalation signs that mean the infection may have climbed to the kidneys: fever, chills, flank or back pain, and nausea. That's a same-day medical call, not a wait-and-see. The same goes for UTI symptoms in pregnancy, in men, or in anyone with diabetes or a weakened immune system.</p><p>For the roughly one in four women who get recurrences, evidence supports drinking more fluids (an extra 1.5 liters daily cut recurrences significantly in trials), urinating after sex, avoiding spermicides, and vaginal estrogen after menopause. Cranberry products show a modest preventive effect in some studies — reasonable to try, not a substitute for the strategies above or, when needed, a clinician-guided preventive antibiotic plan.</p>",
        "author": "hannah-bell-curtis", "reviewer": "danielle-hart-md",
        "published": "2024-07-08", "reviewed": "2024-07-12",
    },
    {
        "title": "Hypothyroidism: Small Gland, Big Fatigue — and a Very Fixable Problem",
        "slug": "hypothyroidism-fatigue", "section": "conditions",
        "subcategory": "Hormones",
        "summary": "An underactive thyroid develops so gradually that many people blame age or stress for years. One blood test settles it.",
        "body_html": "<p>The thyroid sets the idle speed for nearly every organ, so when it slows, everything slows: energy, digestion, heart rate, mood, even the skin's oil production. Because hypothyroidism creeps in over years — fatigue, weight gain, cold intolerance, constipation, dry skin, brain fog — it hides easily inside busy lives. In the U.S. the usual cause is Hashimoto's thyroiditis, an autoimmune process that gradually quiets the gland.</p><p>Diagnosis is one of medicine's easier calls: a TSH blood test, confirmed with a free T4. Treatment is equally direct — daily levothyroxine, a synthetic version of the hormone your thyroid isn't making. Dosing is fine-tuned by TSH every six to eight weeks at first; most people feel the fog lift within a month or two of reaching the right dose.</p><p>The fine print is about absorption. Take levothyroxine on an empty stomach with water, 30 to 60 minutes before breakfast, and keep calcium, iron, and antacids at least four hours away. Don't switch brands or skip lab checks casually — this hormone is dosed in micrograms, and small drifts show up as symptoms.</p>",
        "author": "leah-sandoval", "reviewer": "robert-yuen-md",
        "published": "2024-06-24", "reviewed": "2024-06-28",
    },
    {
        "title": "Stroke: Why 'Time Is Brain' Should Change What You Do in the First Five Minutes",
        "slug": "stroke-time-is-brain", "section": "conditions",
        "subcategory": "Brain & Nerves",
        "summary": "Nearly 2 million brain cells die each minute a stroke goes untreated. The single most important treatment decision is made by bystanders, not doctors.",
        "body_html": "<p>Stroke treatment is a race with a stopwatch running: clot-dissolving medication works within 4.5 hours of symptom onset, and mechanical clot removal within a somewhat longer window for eligible patients — but every minute of delay costs brain tissue. The people who determine whether a patient makes those windows are whoever is standing nearby when it starts.</p><p>The F.A.S.T. check takes ten seconds. Face: ask for a smile — does one side droop? Arms: raise both — does one drift down? Speech: repeat a simple sentence — slurred or strange? Time: any one sign means call 911 now. Don't drive yourself, don't call a relative first, and don't wait to see if it passes — paramedics alert the stroke team before arrival, saving precious minutes.</p><p>A transient episode that resolves — a TIA — is a warning shot, not a reprieve; stroke risk is highest in the following days. Afterward, prevention is aggressive for good reason: blood pressure control, statins such as atorvastatin, antiplatelet therapy with aspirin or clopidogrel, and anticoagulants such as apixaban when atrial fibrillation is the culprit, together cut recurrence risk dramatically.</p>",
        "author": "rachel-whitmore", "reviewer": "anil-bhatt-md",
        "published": "2024-11-18", "reviewed": "2024-11-22",
    },

